        "original_id": "a4f7d605-8388-5cd4-b8bd-5272dd763740",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Eugene O'Neill, Russell Edson, Gus Edson, Brutus Jones\n- Works: The Emperor Jones\n- Roles: playwright, Nobel laureate in Literature, poet, novelist, writer, illustrator, cartoonist-screenwriter, Pullman porter, emperor\n- Topics: American literature, drama\n\nFacts:\n- Eugene O'Neill was born on October 16, 1888, and he became an American playwright and a Nobel laureate in Literature.\n- The Emperor Jones is a 1920 play by American dramatist Eugene O'Neill.\n- In The Emperor Jones, Brutus Jones is a resourceful, self-assured African American and former Pullman porter who kills another black man in a dice game, is jailed, escapes, and sets himself up as emperor on a small backward Caribbean island.\n- Russell Edson was born in 1935, was an American poet, novelist, writer, and illustrator, and was the son of cartoonist-screenwriter Gus Edson.\n- Russell Edson died on April 29, 2014.\n- Eugene O'Neill died on November 27, 1953.",
